# Chapter 6 · The United Nations and Women

> The syllabus asks for "United Nations Conferences on Women". No descriptive question has yet been set on them, but the MCQs love them: the first conference (**Mexico**), the fourth (**Beijing**), the year of the Platform for Action (**1995**), CEDAW (**1996** for Pakistan; "an international bill of rights for women"), and Human Rights Day (**10 December**). This chapter gives you the whole UN story.

## 6.1  The foundations

- The **UN Charter** (==1945==) affirms in its preamble "the **equal rights of men and women**". Women delegates, including those from Latin America, fought to get these words in.
- The **Commission on the Status of Women (CSW)** was set up on ==21 June 1946== as a body of the Economic and Social Council. It still meets every March in New York.
- The **Universal Declaration of Human Rights** was adopted on ==10 December 1948== — the date is now **Human Rights Day** (MCQ 2018). It says rights belong to "everyone", without distinction of sex.
- The **Convention on the Political Rights of Women** (==1952==) and the **Declaration on the Elimination of Discrimination against Women** (==1967==) prepared the way for CEDAW.

## 6.2  The four World Conferences on Women

The UN declared ==1975== **International Women's Year** and ==1976–1985== the **UN Decade for Women**, under the theme "**Equality, Development and Peace**".

::: table | The four UN World Conferences on Women.
@widths 2.6,1.4,10
Conference | Year | What it did
Mexico City (First) | ==1975== | The first world conference on women (MCQ 2016); adopted a **World Plan of Action**; launched the UN Decade for Women; led to UNIFEM and INSTRAW. Tensions between Western women (equality) and women of the South (development, peace)
Copenhagen (Second) | ==1980== | Mid-Decade review; **CEDAW opened for signature** in a ceremony; stressed **employment, health and education**; found a gap between rights on paper and women's ability to use them
Nairobi (Third) | ==1985== | End of the Decade; adopted the **Forward-Looking Strategies** to the year 2000; the NGO forum drew thousands of women, many from Africa and the South
Beijing (Fourth) | ==1995== | Adopted the **Beijing Declaration and Platform for Action** (MCQs 2019, 2024, 2026) — **189** governments; **12 critical areas of concern**; **gender mainstreaming** endorsed; a huge NGO forum at Huairou
:::

::: fig timeline | The UN road for women's rights.
@layout vertical
1945: UN Charter — "equal rights of men and women"
1946: Commission on the Status of Women
1948: Universal Declaration of Human Rights (10 December)
1975: International Women's Year; Mexico City conference
1979: CEDAW adopted (in force 1981)
1980: Copenhagen conference
1985: Nairobi — Forward-Looking Strategies
1993: Vienna — "women's rights are human rights"; Declaration on Violence against Women
1994: Cairo population conference (ICPD)
1995: Beijing — Platform for Action
2000: Security Council Resolution 1325; Beijing+5; Millennium Development Goals
2010: UN Women created
2015: Sustainable Development Goals — SDG 5
2025: Beijing+30
:::

## 6.3  Beijing 1995 — the most important conference

::: case The Fourth World Conference on Women, Beijing (4–15 September 1995) | the landmark
Governments of **189** countries adopted the **Beijing Declaration and Platform for Action**, still the most complete global plan for women's rights. The phrase "**women's rights are human rights**" — used earlier at Vienna in 1993 — became the conference's message. Prime Minister **Benazir Bhutto** of Pakistan addressed the conference.
:::

::: table | Beijing's 12 critical areas of concern.
@widths 1.2,5.8,1.2,5.8
Code | Area | Code | Area
A | Women and poverty | G | Women in power and decision-making
B | Education and training of women | H | Institutional mechanisms for the advancement of women
C | Women and health | I | Human rights of women
D | Violence against women | J | Women and the media
E | Women and armed conflict | K | Women and the environment
F | Women and the economy | L | The girl child
:::

- Area **K** is why a 2023 MCQ said the **Beijing** conference identified women's vital role in **environmental management**.
- Beijing endorsed **gender mainstreaming**: assessing the effect on women and men of every policy, in every sector.
- The **reviews**: Beijing+5 (New York, ==2000==), +10 (==2005==), +15 (==2010==), +20 (==2015==), +25 (==2020==) and **+30** (==2025==), reviewed at the CSW each time. The Beijing+30 review found real progress in girls' education and laws, but warned of slow change in power, persistent violence and a backlash against women's rights in many countries.

## 6.4  CEDAW — the international bill of rights for women

::: law CEDAW | adopted 18 December 1979 · in force 3 September 1981
The **Convention on the Elimination of All Forms of Discrimination against Women** defines discrimination against women (Article 1) and requires states to end it in **law, politics, education, work, health, marriage and family life**. States report every four years to the **CEDAW Committee** of 23 experts. An **Optional Protocol** (==1999==) lets individuals complain to the Committee; Pakistan has not joined it.
-> A 2026 MCQ: CEDAW is often described as **an international bill of rights for women**.
:::

::: table | Key articles of CEDAW.
@widths 2,12
Article | Content
1 | Definition of discrimination against women
2 | States must condemn discrimination and change laws, customs and practices
4 | **Temporary special measures** (such as quotas) are not discrimination
5 | Change **social and cultural patterns** and stereotypes of women's inferiority
7 | Equality in political and public life
10 | Equality in education
11 | Equality in employment
12 | Equality in health care, including family planning
14 | **Rural women**
16 | Equality in marriage and family relations
29 | Settlement of disputes between states (the International Court of Justice)
:::

::: pakistan | Pakistan and CEDAW
Pakistan **acceded** to CEDAW on ==12 March 1996== (MCQs 2016, 2018, 2019). It made a **general declaration** that its accession is subject to the provisions of the **Constitution**, and a **reservation** to **Article 29(1)** (disputes going to the International Court of Justice). Pakistan reports periodically to the CEDAW Committee, which has repeatedly urged it to repeal discriminatory laws, enforce laws against violence and child marriage, and raise women's economic and political participation.
:::

## 6.4a  CEDAW in Pakistani courts and policy

CEDAW is not automatically part of Pakistani law — a treaty must be implemented by legislation. But Pakistani courts have cited it when interpreting women's constitutional rights, and Parliament has referred to it when passing laws on harassment, acid attacks and violence. The **National Commission on the Status of Women** monitors Pakistan's compliance, and NGOs prepare "**shadow reports**" to the CEDAW Committee giving their own account alongside the government's.

::: simple
CEDAW works like a **report card**. Every few years Pakistan must explain to a UN committee what it has done for women; activists send their own version; the committee publishes its comments. It cannot punish — but it keeps the questions coming.
:::

## 6.5  After Beijing: the wider UN agenda

- **Vienna** World Conference on Human Rights (==1993==) and the **Declaration on the Elimination of Violence against Women** (DEVAW, December ==1993==).
- **Cairo** International Conference on Population and Development (==1994==) — reproductive rights and men's responsibility.
- **Security Council Resolution 1325** (==31 October 2000==) on **Women, Peace and Security** — women's role in preventing conflict and building peace.
- **Millennium Development Goals** (==2000==) — Goal 3: gender equality; Goal 5: maternal health.
- **UN Women** (created by the General Assembly in July ==2010==; operational from ==2011==; first head **Michelle Bachelet**) merged four earlier UN bodies, including UNIFEM.
- **Sustainable Development Goals** (==2015==) — **SDG 5**: "Achieve gender equality and empower all women and girls", with targets on discrimination, violence, child marriage, unpaid care, leadership and reproductive rights.
- **HeForShe** (==2014==) and the **Generation Equality Forum** (==2021==).

## 6.5a  The NGO forums and the voices of the South

Alongside each government conference, an **NGO forum** gathered activists: about 6,000 at Mexico City (==1975==), about 14,000 at Nairobi (==1985==) and about 30,000 at **Huairou**, near Beijing (==1995==). At Mexico City, women from the South argued that for them **development, peace and an end to colonialism and apartheid** mattered as much as legal equality; Western feminists learnt that "women's issues" were not the same everywhere. By Nairobi and Beijing, networks such as **DAWN** (Development Alternatives with Women for a New Era, founded ==1984==) had made Southern women's voices central.

## 6.5b  Criticisms of CEDAW and Beijing

1. **Reservations.** Many states — including several Muslim-majority states — made broad reservations to key articles (such as Article 2 or 16), which the CEDAW Committee says weaken the treaty.
2. **No enforcement.** The Committee can only review reports and recommend.
3. **Cultural objections.** Some critics call the agenda Western; defenders point out that states negotiated and adopted it themselves.
4. **Implementation gaps.** Beijing+30 found that no country had achieved full equality.

## 6.6  Assessment of the UN's role

::: fig compare | The UN and women: achievements and limits.
## Achievements
- Set global standards: CEDAW, Beijing, SDG 5
- Made violence against women a human-rights issue
- Created national machineries and data
- Gave women's NGOs a world stage
## Limits
- No power to enforce; states can ignore reports
- Broad reservations weaken CEDAW
- Implementation gaps in poor countries
- Seen by critics as imposing Western values
:::

::: pakistan | What the UN process gave Pakistan
The UN process shaped Pakistan's women's machinery: a **Women's Division** (==1979==), the **Beijing** follow-up **National Plan of Action** (==1998==), the **National Commission on the Status of Women** (==2000==), the **National Policy for Development and Empowerment of Women** (==2002==), gender-reform programmes and the reserved seats of ==2002==. CEDAW reporting also gives Pakistani activists a regular international platform to press the state.
:::
